UID:heavens-hospital-next-nature-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261020
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:Deadline: Next Nature: Heaven's Hospital Open Call (commissions on 
 the future of care)
DESCRIPTION:Heaven's Hospital\, a research and presentation platform and ex
 hibition at the Next Nature Museum\, invites designers\, artists\, archite
 cts\, filmmakers\, writers\, technologists\, game developers\, researchers
 \, scientists\, care professionals and other creative makers to develop ne
 w perspectives on the future of care. Starting from the question of how ca
 re environments can transform from repair factories into sensory ecosystem
 s\, the open call The Dreamed-Up Care seeks fresh ideas rather than ready-
 made solutions: speculative designs\, artistic research\, future scenarios
 \, prototypes\, new applications of AI\, architecture\, digital systems\, 
 rituals\, films\, installations\, services and new forms of collaboration.
  TWELVE CARE ISSUES frame the call\, including the shift from illness to h
 ealth\, the hospital as machine\, staff shortages\, AI changing the role o
 f the doctor\, the patient as data producer\, chronic conditions\, mental 
 health\, the missing human experience in hospitals\, care inequality\, cli
 mate and care\, fragmentation of treatment\, and the medicalisation of bir
 th\, old age and dying. WHAT THEY WANT: projects exploring the future of h
 ealthcare through artistic research\, speculative design or technological 
 innovation that actively engage the public (installations\, interactive en
 vironments\, spatial interventions\, digital experiences\, games\, perform
 ances or other participatory forms)\, and cross-disciplinary work combinin
 g design with AI\, robotics\, immersive media\, biotechnology\, synthetic 
 biology\, architecture\, food design\, sound design\, data science\, ecolo
 gy or philosophy. SUPPORT: three selected projects each receive EUR 15\,00
 0 to develop a first prototype or artistic intervention\, become Heaven's 
 Hospital Fellows\, contribute to the ongoing research programme and may be
  publicly presented in the Next Nature Museum. Proposals must be feasible 
 within the available time and budget while remaining ambitious and experim
 ental. WHO CAN APPLY: individuals or collectives. HOW TO APPLY: one propos
 al per participant via the online application portal\, consisting of the c
 ompleted application form\, a CV and a portfolio of maximum 10 pages or sl
 ides\; PDF only\, maximum 4 MB per file\, in English or Dutch. SELECTION C
 RITERIA: originality and imagination\, relevance to the future of healthca
 re\, artistic and design quality\, innovative use of technology\, public e
 ngagement\, feasibility\, and potential contribution to the research progr
 amme. TIMELINE: deadline 20 October 2026\; jury meets November 2026\; sele
 cted participants announced December 2026\; research\, development and pro
 totype production through 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Heaven's Hospital / Next 
 Nature Museum\nLocation: Next Nature Museum\, Eindhoven\, the Netherlands.
  Open internationally\; applications in English or Dutch.\nAward: Three se
 lected projects each receive a budget of EUR 15\,000 for the development o
 f a first prototype or artistic intervention. Selected participants become
  Heaven's Hospital Fellows and join an international network of designers\
 , researchers\, care professionals and artists. No application fee stated.
 \nDuration: Research\, development and prototype production during 2027\, 
 with presentation within the Heaven's Hospital exhibition at the Next Natu
 re Museum.\n\nMore: https://nextnature.org/en/opencall?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nextnature.org/en/opencall?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Next Nature Museum\, Eindhoven\, the Netherlands. Open internation
 ally\; applications in English or Dutch.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
