 sation-grant-scheme?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:Netherlands-based applicants\; funded activity takes place mainly 
 abroad. At least one international partner required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arts-council-england-music-growth-fund-small-strand-2026@artificialnouv
 eau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts Council England: Music Growth Fund\, GBP 1\,000-40\,
 000 strand
DESCRIPTION:The Music Growth Fund is Arts Council England's new investment 
 programme for growth in new music\, meaning original live performance\, so
 und recording or musical work recently or newly created or performed by li
 ving UK-based artists and music creators. It sits inside the wider GBP 45 
 million Music Growth Package. WHO IT IS FOR: music festivals\, music manag
 ers\, music promoters\, music publishers\, record labels\, recording studi
 os\, rehearsal spaces and grassroots music venues\, plus established secto
 r bodies and other sector support organisations that support them. Each ap
 plicant type has its own Applicant Specific Guidance on the Music Growth P
 ackage pages. IMPORTANT: this is business and organisational investment\, 
 not a grant for individual artists to make work. Individual artists\, band
 s\, producers and artist collectives should instead look at the separate M
 usic Creators Growth Fund (GBP 10\,000-20\,000)\, whose guidance lands 1 S
 eptember 2026 with the portal opening 1 October 2026. WHAT TO PROPOSE: how
  investment will be used to grow music businesses and/or the wider music i
 ndustry\, and enhance opportunities for artists and music creators\, indus
 try professionals\, music fans and new audiences. Growth can mean increase
 d reach\, stronger business models\, improved sustainability\, skills deve
 lopment or expanded capacity\, and is judged in the context of each applic
 ant's own scale and ambitions. DECISION CRITERIA: potential for growth\, e
 ffective use of funds\, contribution to a fairer and more inclusive music 
 industry\, and contribution to a climate resilient music industry. BEFORE 
 YOU START: you need an approved profile on the Arts Council grants managem
 ent system before you can submit\, so allow time for that. Arts Council re
 serves the right to change the programme\, so check you are on the latest 
 guidance. THIS STRAND: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, with a GBP 3\,000\,000 
 annual budget for 2026/27 and a 25 percent cash match requirement. ROUNDS 
 FOR 2026/27 (GBP 1\,000-40\,000 strand): Round 1 opens 6 August and closes
  3 September 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 2 opens 4 September and clo
 ses 15 October 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 3 opens 16 October and cl
 oses 26 November 2026\, decision 3 February 2027. Round 4 opens 27 Novembe
 r 2026 and closes 7 January 2027\, decision 9 March 2027. ROUNDS FOR THE G
 BP 40\,001-100\,000 strand: Round 1 opens 7 September and closes 1 October
  2026\, decision 30 November. Round 2 opens 23 November 2026 and closes 15
  January 2027\, decision 8 March 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Arts Council Engla
 nd\nLocation: England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and
  music creators.\nAward: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, from a total annual b
 udget of GBP 3\,000\,000 for this strand in 2026/27. You must find at leas
 t 25 percent of the total project budget as CASH match funding. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: Four rounds across 2026/27\; the next closes 3 Septem
 ber 2026\n\nMore: https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/musi
 c-growth-fund-general-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/music-growth-fund-g
 eneral-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and music
  creators.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acls-digital-justice-grants-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: ACLS Digital Justice Grants 2026-27 (Seed and Development
 )
DESCRIPTION:ACLS Digital Justice Grants fund digital projects in the humani
 ties and interpretive social sciences that critically engage with the inte
 rests and histories of people of colour and other historically marginalise
 d communities\, including Black\, Latinx\, Indigenous\, disabled and LGBTQ
 + communities. The programme's stated aims are to diversify the digital do
 main\, advance justice and equity in digital scholarly practice\, and cont
 ribute to public understanding of racial and social justice issues\, with 
 an explicit interest in scholars working with under-utilised or understudi
 ed source materials and those at institutions with less infrastructure for
  digital work. TWO TRACKS\, one deadline: SEED GRANTS of USD 10\,000 to 25
 \,000 for projects in the start-up or prototyping phase\, covering plannin
 g\, prototyping and methodology testing\; and DEVELOPMENT GRANTS of USD 50
 \,000 to 100\,000 for projects that have moved past proof of concept and c
 an show preliminary work and evidence of engagement. ELIGIBILITY: at least
  one principal investigator must be a scholar in the humanities or interpr
 etive social sciences\, and awarded funds must be administered by a US ins
 titution of higher education. WHAT IS NOT FUNDED: creative works such as n
 ovels or films\, textbooks\, straightforward translations\, purely pedagog
 ical projects\, and institutional indirect costs. Note that outputs are ex
 pected to be made as widely available as intellectual property allows\, id
 eally under the most liberal open-source or Creative Commons licence. TIMI
 NG: applications go through the ACLS online fellowship and grant administr
 ation system (ofa.acls.org) by 3 November 2026 at 17:00 ET\; grant terms b
 egin between 1 July and 31 December 2027 and run 12 to 18 months. The prog
 ramme is funded by the Mellon Foundation.\n\nOrganisation: American Counci
 l of Learned Societies (ACLS)\, funded by the Mellon Foundation\nLocation:
  Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher education.
  No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principal inves
 tigator.\nAward: Seed Grants USD 10\,000 to 25\,000 for start-up and proto
 typing work\; Development Grants USD 50\,000 to 100\,000 for projects past
  proof of concept. Institutional indirect costs are not covered. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Grant terms run 12 to 18 months\, starting between 1
  July 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline 3 November 2026\, 17:00 ET.\n\nM
 ore: https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher edu
 cation. No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principa
 l investigator.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-arts-in-community-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts in Community Grants 2027
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Arts in Community Grants fund artist fees for commu
 nity engagement projects featuring visiting Southern artists. Each funded 
 project must include both a public presentation and an educational or comm
 unity engagement component\, so this is not a straight presenting grant. U
 p to USD 10\,000 is available per project against a dollar-for-dollar cash
  match\, with a minimum artist fee of USD 4\,000 and a minimum request of 
 USD 2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: tax-exempt 501(c)(3) nonprofit arts organizations
 \, official units of local\, county or state government\, and federally re
 cognised tribal communities with nonprofit status\, located in the nine-st
 ate South Arts region (Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\
 , Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee). Applicants
  must have a Unique Entity Identifier (UEI). Applications opened 15 July 2
 026\; applicants are notified by email by 24 November 2026\, and grants ar
 e subject to funding availability. Projects must take place between 1 Janu
 ary 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline Tuesday 1 September 2026 at 11:59p
 m ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: USA\, nine-state South Arts r
 egion: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, 
 North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee.\nAward: Up to USD 10\,000 p
 er project\, covering artist fees. A dollar-for-dollar cash match is requi
 red\, so a USD 10\,000 request needs USD 10\,000 in matching funds. Minimu
 m artist fee is USD 4\,000 and the minimum grant request is USD 2\,000. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Projects must take place between 1 January 20
 27 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportun
 ities/arts-community-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/arts-community-grants?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA\, nine-state South Arts region: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, 
 Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and T
 ennessee.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:epic-megagrants-cycle-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Epic MegaGrants 2026 (Cycle 2)
DESCRIPTION:Epic MegaGrants are financial grants from Epic Games for creati
 ve\, noteworthy and innovative projects built in and around Unreal Engine\
 , or projects that enhance the open source 3D graphics ecosystem. Over 2\,
 000 grants have been awarded to date. The programme is explicitly aimed at
  smaller teams\, solo developers and individuals as well as studios: Epic 
 states that individuals and small teams are eligible and encouraged\, and 
 that it treats every project equally regardless of who you are. WHAT IS FU
 NDED: (1) INDIE GAMES\, small or solo teams working in Unreal Engine\, sub
 mitted past the idea and design phase with at least some working prototype
 \, ideally with a video link\; (2) UEFN EXPERIENCES\, Fortnite Creative is
 lands and interactive experiences built in Unreal Editor for Fortnite\; (3
 ) ENGINE MIGRATION\, a proportion of MegaGrants is reserved for teams movi
 ng a partially developed project from another engine to Unreal\; (4) FILM\
 , TV AND LIVE EVENTS\, including live-action and animated films and shows\
 , broadcast\, live and location-based events\; (5) OTHER NON-GAMES VERTICA
 LS such as architecture\, automotive\, manufacturing\, simulation\, produc
 t design\, advertising and VR/AR\, where more detailed project plans and p
 rototypes are expected\; (6) EDUCATION\, including research\, curricula an
 d course development and university programmes\, though for institutions E
 pic accepts submissions only from degree-granting schools and educators at
  such schools\; (7) TOOLS AND OPEN SOURCE\, creating open source tools or 
 enhancing open source software using Unreal Engine. NON-UNREAL PROJECTS AR
 E ELIGIBLE if you want to move them to Unreal Engine\, or if the project e
 nhances open source 3D content creation whether or not it relates to Unrea
 l Engine. EVALUATION rests on three things: quality and unique appeal (hig
 h-quality demos or builds\, well-documented descriptions\, technical ambit
 ion)\; community benefit (how you will give back\, for example open-sourci
 ng the project\, releasing assets through a marketplace\, sharing learning
 s\, or shipping an SDK\, tool or plug-in)\; and viability (a clear budget 
 with major spend areas and a project plan with milestones\, deliverables a
 nd timeline). NOTE ON DEADLINES: the FAQ still describes MegaGrants as an 
 ongoing programme with no specific deadline\, but the timeline published o
 n the same page now runs in fixed cycles\, and the Cycle 2 submission wind
 ow closes 4 September 2026 at 23:59 ET. Treat the cycle date as the operat
 ive deadline. You may apply for more than one project\, and may reapply fo
 r the same project after a decision if you have made updates. Epic is not 
 offering sponsorships and cannot give feedback on applications. Apply at m
 egagrants.unrealengine.com.\n\nOrganisation: Epic Games\nLocation: Open wo
 rldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to you\, you are 
 eligible.\nAward: Typical grants range from USD 5\,000 to USD 75\,000\, wi
 th some exceptional projects receiving up to USD 150\,000. Epic states tha
 t recipients continue to own their IP and may publish it however they wish
 \, so this is a grant rather than an investment or equity deal. Grants up 
 to and including USD 25\,000 may be paid in a single instalment\; larger g
 rants are paid across several instalments tied to continued progress. Fund
 s are for project and team expenses such as development and hiring\; hardw
 are purchases or rentals are approved only occasionally\, case by case. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Cycle 2 of 2026 runs 29 June to 11 December 2
 026. Submission window 29 June to 4 September 2026 (closes 23:59 ET)\, rev
 iew 5 September to 4 December 2026\, notices 7 to 11 December 2026. Cycle 
 1 ran 12 January to 19 June 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.unrealengine.com/me
 gagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.unrealengine.com/megagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open worldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to
  you\, you are eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:frameline-completion-fund-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261009
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261010
SUMMARY:Deadline: Frameline Completion Fund 2027
DESCRIPTION:Long-running post-production grant from Frameline\, the San Fra
 ncisco organisation behind the world's longest-running LGBTQ+ film festiva
 l\, for films that represent and reflect LGBTQ+ life in all its complexity
  and richness. Grants run up to USD 5\,000\, typically USD 1\,000 to 5\,00
 0\, and the jury may recommend more or less than that range. WHAT IT COVER
 S: any post-production cost\, explicitly including editing\, special effec
 ts and graphics\, music licensing\, photo licensing\, and\, notably\, clos
 ed captioning and audio description\, so accessibility work is fundable ra
 ther than an afterthought. STAGE GATE\, the thing that disqualifies most a
 pplicants: your project must be at least 90 percent complete with a rough 
 cut available\, and in or about to enter post-production. Work still in de
 velopment or mid-production is not eligible. WHO AND WHAT: all formats\, l
 engths\, genres and nationalities are welcome\, spanning documentary\, nar
 rative\, experimental\, animated and episodic work\, so long as the film i
 s about LGBTQ+ people and their communities. Directors must have creative 
 and financial control of the project. NO APPLICATION FEE\, which is worth 
 flagging given how many completion funds charge one. TIMING: the submissio
 n window opens 31 August 2026 and closes 9 October 2026\, with decisions i
 n January 2027. The 2026 cycle's grantees have already been announced ahea
 d of Frameline's 50th festival\, so this is the next round.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Frameline\nLocation: Administered from San Francisco\, United States. O
 pen to filmmakers of all nationalities.\nAward: Grants of up to USD 5\,000
 \, with a standard range of USD 1\,000 to 5\,000\; the jury can recommend 
 amounts above or below that range. No application fee.\nDuration: Submissi
 on window opens 31 August 2026 and closes 9 October 2026\, with notificati
 on in January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.frameline.org/artist-development/
 completion-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.frameline.org/artist-development/completion-fund?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Administered from San Francisco\, United States. Open to filmmaker
 s of all nationalities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nalip-director-incubator-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: Netflix x NALIP Director Incubator 2026-27 (short film gr
 ants)
DESCRIPTION:The Netflix x NALIP Director Incubator is a career acceleration
  program supporting emerging and mid-career directors in creating bold\, o
 riginal short films centered on stories from women's perspectives. Four di
 rectors each receive a USD 45\,000 production grant to make an original na
 rrative short\, along with individualized guidance\, career development op
 portunities\, and the chance to showcase their work to industry leaders. E
 LIGIBILITY: emerging or mid-career film or television directors based in t
 he United States who are current NALIP members in good standing\, availabl
 e for all program activities\, and applying with an original narrative sho
 rt film project. APPLICATION: online application plus a director's stateme
 nt\, project synopsis\, resume or CV\, and portfolio links. TIMELINE: appl
 ications open 1 September 2026\; deadline 21 September 2026\; review mid-O
 ctober\; finalist interviews late October\; fellows announced November 202
 6.\n\nOrganisation: NALIP (National Association of Latino Independent Prod
 ucers) with Netflix\nLocation: United States. Applicants must be US-based 
 and a current NALIP member in good standing.\nAward: USD 45\,000 productio
 n grant for each of four selected directors to create an original narrativ
 e short film\, plus individualized guidance\, career development opportuni
 ties\, and an industry showcase. No application fee\, but current NALIP me
 mbership is required to apply.\nDuration: Fellows announced November 2026\
 ; short films produced through the incubator cycle.\n\nMore: https://www.n
 alip.org/director_incubator?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.nalip.org/director_incubator?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Applicants must be US-based and a current NALIP mem
 ber in good standing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asf-fellowships-americans-nordic-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: American-Scandinavian Foundation: Fellowships for America
 ns in the Nordic Countries 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:The American-Scandinavian Foundation's award program for study 
 and research abroad is its longest-standing commitment to educational exch
 ange: over the past 100 years\, more than 4\,000 fellowships and grants ha
 ve gone to Americans working in the Nordic region and Scandinavians in the
  US engaged in study\, research\, or creative arts projects. Fellowships f
 or Americans in the Nordic Countries support projects in Denmark\, the Far
 oe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi. 
 AMOUNTS: long-term fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 months\, a
 nd short-term grants of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months\; awards are gi
 ven in all fields\, with graduate students (preferably dissertation-relate
 d)\, academic professionals\, and creative artists eligible. In 2026\, 31 
 American Fellows were granted over USD 277\,390 in total. REPORTING: fello
 ws abroad more than six months submit an interim report mid-project\, and 
 all fellows submit a final narrative report within 60 days of the fellowsh
 ip period's end. DEADLINE: 1 November 2026 for the 2027-28 cycle. See ASF'
 s Program Guidelines for full requirements and apply via the ASF applicati
 on portal.\n\nOrganisation: American-Scandinavian Foundation\nLocation: No
 rdic region (Denmark\, Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Nor
 way\, Sweden\, and Sapmi). For American graduate students\, academic profe
 ssionals\, and creative artists\; project must take place in the Nordic co
 untries.\nAward: Long-term fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 mo
 nths\; short-term grants of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months. In 2026\, 
 31 American Fellows received over USD 277\,390 in total funding. Awards ar
 e given in all fields\, including creative arts projects.\nDuration: 1 to 
 3 months (grants) or 4 to 12 months (fellowships)\; year-long fellows subm
 it an interim report\, and all fellows submit a final narrative report wit
 hin 60 days of the fellowship's end.\n\nMore: https://www.amscan.org/fello
 wships-grants/grants-and-awards-for-americans/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
