DESCRIPTION:Rhizome's annual microgrants\, running since 2014\, fund new cr
 eative projects related to digital art and networked culture\, especially 
 web-based works. Past awardees include a multilingual archive of printed "
 Internet directory" books\, a browser-based adventure game about caring fo
 r "small but alive" creatures\, and a free pop-up school in Portland. THIS
  YEAR'S CATEGORIES: PERMANENCE\, with Larva Labs (digital projects built t
 o endure)\; MISUSE (projects that misuse the technologies they depend on)\
 ; HANDMADE (non-AI works and handmade digital practices)\; and CO-OP (cult
 ural artifacts that result from collective online behaviour). TERRELL DAVI
 S FUND: with Joygill Moriah and Liz Mputu (LVLZ Healing Hub)\, Rhizome off
 ers additional grants honouring the legacy of digital artist and curator T
 errell Davis\, for work speaking to Black and Afrodiasporic experiences\; 
 projects in any category may opt in. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, hackers\, re
 searchers\, game masters\, designers\, storytellers\, tinkerers\, communit
 y builders\, scientists\, archivists and culture workers\, regardless of t
 echnical or demographic background. JURY: Rhizome staff with guest jurors 
 Larva Labs and Natalie Stone\, Joygill Moriah\, Herdimas Anggara and Annie
  Rau. DEADLINE: 20 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Rhizome\nLocation: Onli
 ne. Open call for web-based and networked projects\; no location requireme
 nt is stated in the announcement.\nAward: USD 500 to 1\,500 per project. A
 dditional annual grants through the Terrell Davis fund (with Joygill Moria
 h and LVLZ Healing Hub) for work speaking to Black and Afrodiasporic exper
 iences\; projects in any category can opt in.\nDuration: Per project.\n\nM
 ore: https://microgrants.rhizome.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
